  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 152 AM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nNo major changes, please see previous discussion...\n\nTuesday night through the end of the work week could see a few areas \nof showers trying to lift up into the region, but this remains \nlargely uncertain and low confidence as models remain split on the \nevolution of low pressure to our south and how far north moisture \ncan make it. While some ensembles have begun to push the low and \nmoisture associated with what will be the remnants of Hurricane Polo \nfurther northwards compared to the last few runs, overall this \nsystem still looks to be too far south to bring any further \nappreciable moisture to our area beyond Tuesday outside of maybe our \nfarthest southeast zones, primarily Cheyenne County in the Nebraska \nPanhandle. The NBM continues to keep us high dry as well, with the \nabove scenario remaining in the grids as of this writing. Still \nthrough Thursday POP chances aren't completely zero, but outside of \nthe I-80 corridor and southward it's not looking favorable for any \nmeaningful moisture in the long term. Otherwise we'll see a very \nslight warming trend on Wednesday, with temperatures warming a few \ndegrees with widespread 60's and a few 70's expected, but a second \ncold airmass should drop us right back down into the widespread 60's \non Thursday. Friday and Saturday high pressure over the southwestern \nUS will allow for a warming and drying trend, returning us to \nseveral degrees above normal with highs in the 70's across the \nregion. Sunday flow turns more northwesterly and a glancing upper \nlevel trough will help push a weak cold front into the region, \nbringing temperatures back down to near normal, but a lack of \nnotable moisture will keep our precipitation chances almost nil and \nprovide little more than a brief cool down to end the long term \nforecast.",
